WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.280
Hi. And welcome to
our fifth enterprise community meetup,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.480
where we'll discuss another end
to end data science workflow

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.105
using Posit Team. My
name is Ryan Johnson,

00:00:11.105 --> 00:00:13.925
and I'm a data science
advisor here at Posit.

00:00:14.625 --> 00:00:15.825
Now for today's session,

00:00:15.825 --> 00:00:17.920
we're gonna do things a
little bit differently.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.160
And rather than discuss a single
end to end data science workflow,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.200
we're actually gonna break it
up into a series of smaller

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.945
workflows using one of my
favorite packages in both r and

00:00:28.945 --> 00:00:30.565
Python called Pins.

00:00:31.345 --> 00:00:32.545
Now during today's session,

00:00:32.545 --> 00:00:35.820
we'll start off with a
brief introduction into Posit Team,

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.740
and then we'll talk about
how Posit Team, specifically Posit

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.320
Connect can be used to
enhance your pin's workflows.

00:00:42.915 --> 00:00:43.635
So with that,

00:00:43.635 --> 00:00:47.575
let's go ahead and get started
with an introduction to Posit Team.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.795
Alright. If you joined
us in the past months,

00:00:51.795 --> 00:00:54.390
then you probably seen
this diagram before.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.950
This is a quick
overview of Posit Team.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.670
And we'll start right up here
at the very top with your data

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.625
scientists, your data
analysts, your end users.

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:06.625
And they will be creating
insights using Posit Workbench.

00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:08.245
This is a development
environment,

00:01:08.785 --> 00:01:10.820
and they can develop
within RStudio, Jupyter, or

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.500
VSCode, creating things
in both R and Python.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.940
So for your R developers,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.215
maybe they're creating shiny
applications, quarto documents, plumber APIs,

00:01:22.995 --> 00:01:24.675
or if you have
Python developers,

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:27.555
maybe they're creating
applications using things like

00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:29.190
Bokeh or Dash, streamlit.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.330
You can have Jupyter notebooks
as well, shiny for Python,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.990
pins for Python, which we'll
talk about in a bit here.

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:39.635
But it doesn't do the
data scientists any good if they

00:01:39.635 --> 00:01:42.995
can't share that content with the
people that need to see it.

00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:46.260
That could be their coworkers,
it could be their boss,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.340
or maybe just their
friends and family,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.380
and that's where Posit
Connect comes into play.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.665
Connect is our professional
publishing platform and allows

00:01:54.665 --> 00:01:58.745
you to easily share those
insights with whoever you want.

00:01:58.745 --> 00:02:01.545
And then finally, we have
Posit Package Manager,

00:02:01.545 --> 00:02:03.705
which does exactly
as this name implies,

00:02:03.705 --> 00:02:08.510
help control all those great open
source packages in both r and Python.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:13.705
And you can also host and
share any internally developed r and

00:02:13.705 --> 00:02:16.125
Python packages with
your team as well.

00:02:17.785 --> 00:02:22.290
Now for today's workflow, we're gonna
focus on pins. And in a second,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.810
I'll explain what the pins
package is and how you use it,

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.555
but I thought it might be
helpful to first describe a few

00:02:29.555 --> 00:02:32.595
scenarios where you might want
to consider using the pins

00:02:32.595 --> 00:02:34.135
package for your workflows.

00:02:35.475 --> 00:02:38.660
In this first scenario, Have
you ever created an asset,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.180
maybe like a piece of data
that you thought was the final

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.700
version of the data,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.685
but then you had to go back
and make some edits and create

00:02:46.685 --> 00:02:48.525
another final version.

00:02:48.525 --> 00:02:51.085
And rather than overwriting
the original dataset,

00:02:51.085 --> 00:02:53.350
you decide to give
it a new name.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.870
And maybe you go through this
process again and again and again,

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:00.815
and you end up with multiple
final versions of a data set.

00:03:00.815 --> 00:03:04.195
And no one knows what the
true final version is.

00:03:06.575 --> 00:03:10.300
Maybe this data we just talked
about is used by many different

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:14.220
content types, including a shiny
app in r, or a quarto

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.500
document in Python,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.175
and you're worried that the
data may not be consistent

00:03:18.175 --> 00:03:19.555
across all assets.

00:03:21.055 --> 00:03:21.935
Additionally,

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:25.295
maybe you want to share
this data with multiple people,

00:03:25.295 --> 00:03:29.460
and it's typically much easier
and safer to share a pin

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.720
rather than trying to
email files around.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.585
And finally, maybe this
data contains sensitive information,

00:03:36.585 --> 00:03:40.105
and you only want to share
it with specific people or groups

00:03:40.105 --> 00:03:44.240
of people. And this is actually
possible when using pins in

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.340
combination with Posit Connect.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.395
So how do pins actually work?

00:03:50.035 --> 00:03:53.475
The best way to think of pins
in my opinion is to think of a

00:03:53.475 --> 00:03:57.720
cork board similar to how you can
pin a note to a cork board,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.560
you can pin an R or Python
object to a board where

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.140
you and others can access it.

00:04:05.195 --> 00:04:07.455
To further explain this
cork board analogy,

00:04:07.995 --> 00:04:12.075
the push pin is the tool we
use to pin an object to the cork

00:04:12.075 --> 00:04:14.175
board. This is
the pins package.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:19.210
The note that we pin is our
R or Python object, and this

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:22.910
could be a data set, a
model, plots, or even files.

00:04:23.525 --> 00:04:24.165
Finally,

00:04:24.165 --> 00:04:27.525
the cork board in our workflows
for today will be Posit

00:04:27.525 --> 00:04:30.885
Connect. Now there are a lot
of other boards you could use,

00:04:30.885 --> 00:04:35.440
including Amazon S3, Azure,
or Google Cloud Storage, or

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.435
even local or shared folders.
But Posit Connect does come

00:04:39.435 --> 00:04:42.015
with some added advantages,
which we'll discuss today.

00:04:43.675 --> 00:04:45.515
Before we get
started with pins,

00:04:45.515 --> 00:04:48.450
we need to do a small
amount of prep work.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:52.290
And since we are going to show
some workflows in both R and Python,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.255
we'll first need to install
the pins package in both languages,

00:04:56.255 --> 00:04:58.335
and then we'll need to
connect to our board,

00:04:58.335 --> 00:05:01.935
which again will be Posit
Connect. So let's do that now,

00:05:01.935 --> 00:05:04.035
and we'll start
with r in RStudio.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.140
So here I am within
Posit workbench,

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.880
and the first thing I'm
going to do is log in.

00:05:15.105 --> 00:05:17.345
Now that we're within
Posit Workbench,

00:05:17.345 --> 00:05:21.685
the first thing I'm going to do is
